<div class="case-method-detail">
  <h3>Sampling rule</h3>
  <div class="case-equation-scroll" tabindex="0" aria-label="Unite and Conquer sampling equations">
    <p role="math">
      p<sub>0</sub>(z<sub>t</sub>) ∝ ∏<sub>j</sub> p<sub>δⱼ</sub>(z<sub>t</sub> | φ)<sup>aⱼ</sup>, &nbsp; ∑<sub>j</sub> a<sub>j</sub> = 1
    </p>
    <p role="math">
      ε<sub>comp</sub> = ∑<sub>i</sub> w<sub>i</sub> ε<sub>i</sub>(z<sub>t</sub>, x<sub>i</sub>, t) − (∑<sub>i</sub> w<sub>i</sub> − 1) ∑<sub>j</sub>
      a<sub>j</sub> ε<sub>j</sub>(z<sub>t</sub>, φ, t), &nbsp; w<sub>i</sub> ≥ 1
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    The null condition is φ. Reliability weights a<sub>j</sub> combine the experts’ unconditional estimates. Guidance strengths w<sub>i</sub> control
    the individual conditions. They serve different roles. With all guidance strengths equal to one, the rule subtracts N − 1 copies of the shared
    unconditional estimate. For one expert, it reduces to classifier-free guidance. See Equations 5–9 in the paper.
  </p>
</div>
